[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says the desk merged every date report into one issue with one fix
queued. It holds three problems, and each needs its own fix. Shipping the queued parser fix closes
the biggest one and leaves the other two open, with their reporters still waiting under a ticket
marked known.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Three problems, each now its own ticket. Do not close #4471 when the parser fix ships.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable
  ticket={4471}
  issues={{
    parser: 'https://github.com/Suimrah/what-it-shows/issues/1',
    hint: 'https://github.com/Suimrah/what-it-shows/issues/2',
    'one-day': 'https://github.com/Suimrah/what-it-shows/issues/3',
  }}
/>

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first, and every tenant on file writes the
  day first. The date comes out with day and month swapped.
- **`hint`**: booking-v3's form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what it says,
  and the form then reads the date day first. Fixing the parser does not touch this.
- **`one-day`**: the date moves by one day, as with a client in Lisbon getting Tuesday and a client in Toronto
  getting Monday. A month/day swap cannot do that. The desk had already read these reports this
  way (`desk-notes.json`) but filed them under the parser bug by tenant. Probably a booking sent
  without its time zone; an engineer has to confirm.

## How the reports were placed

- **One report, one count.** The export pages overlap, so some reports appear twice. Counted once
  by id (`uniqueById`).
- **By tenant, not by intake tag.** Intake tags a report from the reporter's browser locale. The
  cause depends on the tenant's locale and booking form (`causeOf` in `causes.ts`). The two
  disagree in both directions, so the tag is not a count.
- **One-day moves come out first**, whatever the tenant, because no swap explains them.

For comparison, the desk's own split by cause, with the one-day moves still counted inside:

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## Why one ticket hid three

Triage merges on subject line alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
Every one of these reporters wrote some version of "wrong day", so every report landed in one ticket.
The decision accepted that cost for reports with *different* subjects about one bug. It did not
cover the reverse: *one* subject about different bugs. That is this ticket.

## Lessons

- A big ticket can be many bugs with one symptom. Count by cause before you mark it known.
- Group reports by the tenant's locale and form, not by subject or intake tag.
- Decision 0001 and the triage page have not been re-read since 2024. Their rule produced this ticket.

